<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pont du Gard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pont du Gard is a three-tiered Roman aqueduct bridge over the Gardon river in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, built in the 1st century CE to carry water some 50 km to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nemausus</a> (Nîmes). About 49 metres high, it is the tallest of all Roman aqueduct bridges and one of the best preserved, and it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pont-du-gard.jpg" alt="Pont du Gard" width="320" height="123" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benh LIEU SONG (Flickr) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pont_du_Gard_BLS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
